Z||000006||theogre||06-11-99||03:10 PM||||The muffler shop is correct. It is a federal law and fines are very stiff. <p>If I remember correctly this law applies to all cars in the U.S. after modle year 1973. While some states, like PA, are currently fighting with the EPA about how emisions testing will be done the antimodification law is still in effect and enforcable. It also supersedes weeker state laws that may still be on the books. It applies to shops and individuals. <p>As far as I can tell, PA inspectors who pass a domestic car built after 1973 without a catalyst are in violation of existing PA and Federal laws even without the emisions test up the tail pipe. Penalties for this can include jail time, loss of inspection station status for the business, large fines, and other such things.<p>The facts are that a well designed catalyst will do it's job with minimal performance penalty. A large part of the problem with the stock one is that they are just plain old. They are worn, full of dirt, and posibly poisoned with oil antifreeze and other chemicals. Once they get like that the restriction rises sharply.<p>This is why it is very important to make sure that ALL oil burning or antifreeze leak problems are eliminated Before replacing a catalyst. Especially if you are going for an expensive after market one. If any above normal oil loss is occuring thru valve seals, rings, or gaskets, then you will simply destroy the new catalyst. Antifreeze leaks into the engine will have the same result.
Z||000007||Bruce||06-11-99||08:26 PM||||Thanks, Ogre.<br>I've had virtually every thing sealed, tightened, and replaced. I ordered a flowmaster and catco converter to eliminate as much back pressure as possible. New K&N and side scoop make my 86 GT move right along. Cooling is the key to this engine!
Z||000008||theogre||06-12-99||03:59 PM||||cooling is key to all fiero performance but that's another thread. :)
